WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.870
We have this feeling that something is wrong
with our Standard Model.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:10.719
And we have been looking for quite some time
to try to see beyond Standard Model effects.

00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:15.931
We've looked into the behavior of many of
the particles, and, in most of the particles,

00:00:15.931 --> 00:00:20.410
the Standard Model has done a very good job
of describing how those particles will behave.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.330
But the neutrinos are different.

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:26.750
The neutrinos have a beyond-the-Standard-Model
behavior.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:31.039
It comes about because neutrinos turn out
to have mass when in the Standard Model they

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:32.039
were massless.

00:00:32.039 --> 00:00:34.750
This behavior is called neutrino oscillations.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.800
And you can think about this as the neutrinos
doing a little dance as they move along through

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:39.800
space.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.980
They start out as one kind of neutrino — say
what's called the electron neutrino, one type

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:49.570
— and later on they turn into a different
type of neutrino as part of this oscillating

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.120
dance.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.540
It was a very surprising thing for neutrino
physicists to discover.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:00.121
It's a lot like starting out a race that you
only have dogs and at the end you suddenly

00:01:00.121 --> 00:01:04.160
have some cats along with the dogs at the
end.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.760
Neutrino oscillations were a very exciting
discovery.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.550
It won the Nobel Prize in 2015.

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:14.470
And it is our first clue to beyond-the-Standard-Model
physics.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:19.820
And since we started to see new effects within
the neutrino sector, we started looking more

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.990
closely at the neutrino sector.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.750
And we discovered that not only do you have
the oscillations that you could explain by

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:30.330
having the three neutrinos that are in the
Standard Model, there are actually oscillation

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:36.650
effects that appear that are ones that are
not what we would expect if there were only

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.310
three neutrinos.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:44.111
And so one way to try to fix up the problem
is to introduce extra neutrinos into the neutrino

00:01:44.111 --> 00:01:45.111
family.

00:01:45.111 --> 00:01:49.890
Now, if we introduce these extra neutrinos,
then we know that they have to have some of

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.260
the characteristics of neutrinos.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.430
They're fundamental.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.400
They're spin one-half particles and so forth.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:00.260
But it turns out that they cannot be involved
in the weak interaction — otherwise we would

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.770
have seen them already.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:07.670
The weak interaction is weak, but we do actually
see the weak interaction occur.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:11.880
And so they have to have no interactions,
and that's why they're called "sterile."

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.760
It means that these neutrinos, these new kinds
of neutrinos, have no interaction at all.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.780
But they can engage in the dance with their
other neutrino partners and show up in neutrino

00:02:22.781 --> 00:02:23.281
oscillations.

